var e={id:`norganic`,objectives:[`Classify amines as primary, secondary, tertiary, or quaternary; name them by IUPAC and common conventions`,`Prepare ethylamine by reduction of nitriles and phenylamine by reduction of nitrobenzene`,`Account for the relative basicity of ammonia, alkylamines, and phenylamines using inductive and resonance effects`,`Describe and explain the reactions of phenylamine with aqueous bromine and with nitrous acid`,`Synthesise azo dyes by diazonium coupling and explain the colour in terms of conjugated π systems`,`Form amides from acyl chlorides + amines/ammonia and predict products of acid- and base-hydrolysis`,`Describe amino acid structure and explain zwitterion behaviour, isoelectric point, and titration curves`,`Form peptide bonds by condensation; predict the products of acid, base, and enzymatic hydrolysis of proteins`,`Identify nitrogen compounds from a sequence of standard chemical tests`],chapters:[{id:`nc1`,title:`Amines: Structure & Classification`,minutes:8,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`An amine is an organic compound derived from ammonia (NH₃) by replacing one or more H atoms with hydrocarbon groups (alkyl or aryl). The N still keeps a lone pair, which is the source of every reaction we will meet — basicity, nucleophilic attack, diazotisation, and dye coupling all flow from that single non-bonding pair on N.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`CLASSIFICATION OF AMINES`,subtitle:`Classify by the number of carbon groups bonded directly to N.`,blocks:[{type:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`General form`,`Example`],rows:[[`1° primary`,`R–NH₂`,`CH₃CH₂NH₂`],[`2° secondary`,`R–NH–R′`,`(CH₃)₂NH`],[`3° tertiary`,`R–N(R′)–R″`,`(CH₃)₃N`],[`4° quaternary`,`[R₄N]⁺ X⁻`,`(CH₃)₄N⁺Cl⁻`]]},{type:`note`,text:`1°/2°/3° refers to N here, not to a carbon atom as in alcohols/halogenoalkanes.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`1°/2°/3° refers to N — count the C atoms attached to nitrogen`},{type:`h`,text:`Classification`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Primary (1°) amine: N has TWO H + one R. General formula R–NH₂. Example: ethylamine, CH₃CH₂NH₂.`,`Secondary (2°): N has ONE H + two R groups. General formula R–NH–R'. Example: dimethylamine, (CH₃)₂NH.`,`Tertiary (3°): N has NO H + three R groups. General formula R₃N. Example: trimethylamine, (CH₃)₃N.`,`Quaternary (4°) ammonium salt: N has FOUR R groups + positive charge. General formula [R₄N]⁺X⁻. Example: tetramethylammonium chloride, (CH₃)₄N⁺Cl⁻.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`warning`,title:`Don't confuse 1°/2°/3° at N with 1°/2°/3° at C`,body:`For alcohols and halogenoalkanes, "primary/secondary/tertiary" describes the carbon. For amines, it describes the nitrogen — count groups attached to N, not C. They are different conventions; be careful when describing a compound like 2-aminobutane (it is a 2° alcohol... but a 1° amine, because the N still has two H).`},{type:`h`,text:`Naming`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Suffix style: replace the alkane "-e" with "-amine": methanamine, ethanamine, propan-1-amine. (IUPAC 2013 preferred.)`,`Older "-ylamine" style is still acceptable: methylamine, ethylamine, propylamine.`,`Aromatic 1° amines: phenylamine (C₆H₅NH₂), often called aniline. The systematic name is benzenamine but is rarely used in exams.`,`For 2°/3°: list groups alphabetically before "-amine". e.g. N-methylethanamine (CH₃CH₂NHCH₃) — the "N-" specifies that methyl is on the nitrogen, not on a carbon.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Why the lone pair matters`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Basicity: the lone pair accepts H⁺. R₃N: + H⁺ → R₃NH⁺.`,`Nucleophilicity: the lone pair attacks electrophilic C (e.g. C=O of acyl chlorides) → amides.`,`In phenylamine, the lone pair partially delocalises INTO the ring — this single fact explains why the amine is a weak base AND why the ring is highly activated to electrophiles. We will return to this in chapter 3.`]},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Amine = derivative of NH₃; classify by number of groups on N.`,`Names: -amine suffix or -ylamine; phenylamine = C₆H₅NH₂.`,`The N lone pair drives every reaction we will study.`,`1°/2°/3° at N is NOT the same convention as at C — careful.`]}]},{id:`nc2`,title:`Preparation of Amines`,minutes:8,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`There are two preparations the syllabus expects you to recall in detail: (a) reduction of a nitrile to a primary aliphatic amine, and (b) reduction of nitrobenzene to phenylamine. A third route — substitution of a halogenoalkane by ammonia — is mentioned for completeness but is rarely the preferred method because it produces mixtures.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`PREPARATION OF AMINES`,blocks:[{type:`panel`,title:`A · REDUCTION OF NITRILE (+1 C)`,body:[`R–C≡N + 4[H] → R–CH₂–NH₂`,`LiAlH₄/dry ether or H₂/Ni`,`e.g. CH₃CN → CH₃CH₂NH₂`],color:`primary`},{type:`panel`,title:`B · NITROBENZENE → PHENYLAMINE`,body:[`C₆H₅NO₂ + 6[H] → C₆H₅NH₂ + 2H₂O`,`Sn + conc. HCl, reflux; then NaOH`],color:`accent`},{type:`panel`,title:`C · HALOGENOALKANE + NH₃`,body:[`R–X + 2NH₃ → R–NH₂ + NH₄X`,`excess alcoholic NH₃, sealed tube`,`can produce mixtures of 1°/2°/3°/4° products`],color:`pink`},{type:`note`,text:`Routes A and B give cleaner single products and are syllabus-essential.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Three routes — A and B are the syllabus-essential ones`},{type:`h`,text:`Route A — reduction of a nitrile (chain extension by 1 C)`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Equation: R–C≡N + 4[H] → R–CH₂–NH₂.`,`Reagent option 1: LiAlH₄ in dry ether (very strong reducer, anhydrous conditions because LiAlH₄ reacts violently with water).`,`Reagent option 2: H₂(g) with a Ni catalyst at high temperature and pressure (industrial; cheaper than LiAlH₄ for scale).`,`Why this matters synthetically: nitriles are easily made from halogenoalkanes (R–X + KCN/ethanol → R–CN). So R–X → R–CN → R–CH₂–NH₂ extends the carbon chain by one. e.g. CH₃CH₂Br → CH₃CH₂CN → CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂ (3-carbon amine from 2-carbon halide).`]},{type:`h`,text:`Route B — reduction of nitrobenzene to phenylamine`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Equation: C₆H₅–NO₂ + 6[H] → C₆H₅–NH₂ + 2H₂O.`,`Reagents: tin (Sn) or iron (Fe) with concentrated HCl, reflux. The acid is essential — it converts the metal to active reducing species. The reduction proceeds through nitrosobenzene (Ar-N=O) and phenylhydroxylamine (Ar-NHOH) intermediates, but you do not need to know these for ZIMSEC.`,`Workup: the initial product is the soluble salt C₆H₅NH₃⁺Cl⁻ (phenylammonium chloride); add NaOH(aq) afterwards to liberate the free amine, which is then steam-distilled out.`,`Industrial method: H₂/Ni catalyst, again at high T,P — cleaner and safer at scale.`,`Significance: nitrobenzene is itself made by nitrating benzene (HNO₃/H₂SO₄). So benzene → nitrobenzene → phenylamine puts an –NH₂ group on a benzene ring — a hugely useful first step in dye and pharmaceutical synthesis.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Route C — halogenoalkane + ammonia (mentioned, not preferred)`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Equation: R–X + 2NH₃ → R–NH₂ + NH₄X.`,`Conditions: excess NH₃ in ethanol, sealed tube, heat.`,`Problem: the product 1° amine is itself nucleophilic and competes with NH₃ for the next halogenoalkane molecule, giving 2° amine, then 3°, then quaternary salt. The result is a mixture.`,`Excess NH₃ pushes towards the 1° amine but the route is rarely clean. Routes A and B are preferred for that reason.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Synthesis lever — chain extension`,body:`Of all the routes, the nitrile route is most useful in multi-step synthesis because it adds a carbon. Whenever a question gives you an alcohol or halogenoalkane and asks for an amine with one more carbon, plan: alcohol → halogenoalkane → nitrile → amine.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`A: R–CN + 4[H] → R–CH₂–NH₂. Use LiAlH₄/dry ether or H₂/Ni. Adds one C.`,`B: C₆H₅NO₂ + 6[H] → C₆H₅NH₂. Use Sn/conc.HCl, then NaOH (or H₂/Ni).`,`C: R-X + NH₃ — gives mixtures, not preferred.`,`Both A and B work because the N–O or N≡C bond is reducible; the C–H bonds in the rest of the molecule are unaffected by mild reducing agents.`]}]},{id:`nc3`,title:`Basicity of Amines`,minutes:9,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`All amines are bases — they accept H⁺ via the N lone pair. But not all amines are equally basic. The trend alkylamine > NH₃ > phenylamine spans about six orders of magnitude in pKb, and the entire reasoning for the syllabus rests on one idea: the AVAILABILITY of the lone pair on N.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`RELATIVE BASICITY · smaller pKb = stronger base`,blocks:[{type:`bars`,yMax:10,items:[{label:`EtNH₂`,value:6.73,valueLabel:`pKb 3.27`,color:`primary`},{label:`MeNH₂`,value:6.64,valueLabel:`pKb 3.36`,color:`primary`},{label:`NH₃`,value:5.25,valueLabel:`pKb 4.75`,color:`accent`},{label:`PhNH₂`,value:.58,valueLabel:`pKb 9.42`,color:`pink`}]},{type:`note`,text:`alkylamine > NH₃ >> phenylamine`,emphasis:!0},{type:`note`,text:`Alkyl groups donate electron density (+I); the phenyl ring delocalises the N lone pair.`}]},caption:`pKb data: smaller pKb = stronger base`},{type:`h`,text:`How an amine acts as a base`},{type:`ul`,items:[`In water: R–NH₂ + H₂O ⇌ R–NH₃⁺ + OH⁻ (the lone pair grabs H⁺, leaving OH⁻ behind).`,`With acid: R–NH₂ + HCl → R–NH₃⁺Cl⁻ (an alkylammonium salt, ionic, water-soluble, often a white solid).`,`Strength is measured by Kb = [R-NH₃⁺][OH⁻] / [R-NH₂], and pKb = −log₁₀ Kb. Smaller pKb = stronger base.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Why alkylamines are stronger than ammonia`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`WHY PHENYLAMINE IS A WEAK BASE`,blocks:[{type:`compare`,left:{title:`ALKYLAMINE`,color:`primary`,body:[`alkyl group has +I effect`,`electron density pushed onto N`,`lone pair MORE available for H⁺`]},right:{title:`PHENYLAMINE`,color:`pink`,body:[`N lone pair delocalises into ring`,`lone pair LESS available for H⁺`,`therefore weaker base`]}},{type:`panel`,title:`Same delocalisation also activates the ring`,body:[`electron-rich ring reacts rapidly with Br₂(aq)`,`2,4,6-tribromination occurs without FeBr₃`],color:`accent`},{type:`note`,text:`One cause gives two consequences: weak basicity + activated aromatic ring.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Alkyl groups push electrons onto N; the aryl ring pulls them away`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Alkyl groups are weakly electron-donating (positive inductive effect, +I). They push electron density TOWARDS the N atom.`,`More electron density on N = more available lone pair = better at accepting H⁺ = stronger base.`,`Methylamine (pKb 3.36) and ethylamine (pKb 3.27) are both about 30× more basic than ammonia (pKb 4.75).`,`In the gas phase the trend continues: 3° > 2° > 1° amine. In aqueous solution, hydrogen-bonding stabilisation of the protonated amine partly cancels the trend, so pKb values for 1°/2°/3° in water are similar — exam answers focus on the simple +I argument.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Why phenylamine is weaker than ammonia`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Phenylamine: pKb ≈ 9.42 — about 10⁵ times WEAKER than methylamine and ~10⁴ weaker than NH₃.`,`Mechanism: the N lone pair is in a p-type orbital that overlaps with the π system of the benzene ring. This delocalises the lone pair INTO the ring, lowering its energy.`,`A delocalised lone pair is much less available to bond H⁺ — it is "tied up" in the aromatic system. So phenylamine is a poor base.`,`Consequence (ring side): the same delocalisation injects electron density INTO the ring. The ring is now strongly activated towards electrophilic substitution — chapter 4 picks this up.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`One cause, two consequences`,body:`Memorise this pattern. Delocalisation of the N lone pair into the benzene ring (1) weakens phenylamine as a base AND (2) activates the ring for halogenation/nitration. The same explanation answers both halves of an exam question.`},{type:`h`,text:`Salt formation — useful chemistry, useful solubility`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Phenylamine is barely soluble in water but dissolves in dilute HCl as the ammonium salt: C₆H₅NH₂ + HCl → C₆H₅NH₃⁺Cl⁻.`,`Ethylamine, in contrast, is fully water-miscible because of strong N–H···O–H hydrogen bonding to water.`,`Ammonium salts can be turned back into the free amine with NaOH(aq): C₆H₅NH₃⁺Cl⁻ + NaOH → C₆H₅NH₂ + NaCl + H₂O.`,`This pH-dependent solubility is exploited industrially to separate amines from non-basic organics: "shake with HCl, run off the aqueous, basify with NaOH, recover the amine".`]},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`alkylamine > NH₃ > phenylamine. Always.`,`Alkyl: +I donates e⁻ → lone pair more available → stronger base.`,`Aryl: lone pair delocalised into ring → less available → weaker base.`,`The same delocalisation activates the ring (relevant in chapter 4).`,`Amines + acid → salts (water-soluble). NaOH liberates the free amine.`]}]},{id:`nc4`,title:`Reactions of Phenylamine`,minutes:8,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Phenylamine has two faces: a weak base at N, and a highly activated benzene ring. Two named reactions in the ZIMSEC syllabus probe each face. Bromination probes the activated ring; reaction with nitrous acid probes the N–H bond and gives access to the rich diazonium chemistry of chapter 5.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`REACTIONS OF PHENYLAMINE · C₆H₅NH₂`,blocks:[{type:`panel`,title:`WITH Br₂(aq)`,body:[`C₆H₅NH₂ + 3Br₂ → 2,4,6-tribromophenylamine + 3HBr`,`room temperature; no catalyst`,`Br₂ decolourised + white precipitate`],color:`primary`},{type:`panel`,title:`WITH HNO₂ / HCl below 10 °C`,body:[`C₆H₅NH₂ + HNO₂ + HCl → C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ + 2H₂O`,`HNO₂ generated from NaNO₂ + HCl`,`benzenediazonium chloride formed`],color:`accent`},{type:`panel`,title:`WARM DIAZONIUM SALT`,body:[`C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ + H₂O → C₆H₅OH + N₂↑ + HCl`,`hydrolysis to phenol; N₂ effervescence`],color:`pink`},{type:`note`,text:`Cold preserves the diazonium salt; warming decomposes it.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Two named reactions: bromine (ring) and HNO₂ (N–H)`},{type:`h`,text:`Reaction 1 — with aqueous bromine`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Equation: C₆H₅NH₂ + 3Br₂ → 2,4,6-tribromophenylamine + 3HBr.`,`Conditions: simply add Br₂(aq) at room temperature. NO halogen carrier (no FeBr₃ or AlBr₃) is required — the ring is so activated that bromine attacks unaided.`,`Observation: the orange Br₂(aq) is decolourised; a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromophenylamine forms.`,`Why three substitutions? The –NH₂ group activates 2,4,6 positions. Each bromination still leaves the ring activated enough for the next. Stopping at mono- or di-substitution is impractical without protecting the N first (e.g. as the amide).`,`Compare with benzene itself: benzene + Br₂ requires FeBr₃ catalyst and gives mainly mono-substituted product. The contrast is the activating effect of –NH₂.`,`Diagnostic value: a white ppt with Br₂(aq) at RT is good evidence for an aromatic 1° amine (or for phenol — distinguish using HNO₂).`]},{type:`h`,text:`Reaction 2 — with nitrous acid (HNO₂) below 10 °C`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Nitrous acid is unstable and is generated in situ: NaNO₂(aq) + HCl(aq) → HNO₂ + NaCl. The mixture must be ICE-COLD throughout (typically 0–5 °C).`,`Equation: C₆H₅NH₂ + HNO₂ + HCl → C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ + 2H₂O.`,`Product: benzenediazonium chloride (sometimes written PhN₂⁺Cl⁻). It is a pale yellow soluble salt — but only stable below 10 °C. Above this it decomposes.`,`Decomposition above 10 °C: C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ + H₂O → C₆H₅OH + N₂↑ + HCl. So warm the salt and you get phenol plus a stream of nitrogen bubbles. This is a useful synthesis of phenol from phenylamine.`,`Mechanism summary: HNO₂ in acidic conditions provides the nitrosating agent NO⁺ (or H₂NO₂⁺). It attacks the N of the amine, and a sequence of proton-transfers and dehydration yields the diazonium ion Ar–N⁺≡N.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Comparing 1° aliphatic vs 1° aromatic amines with HNO₂`},{type:`ul`,items:[`1° aliphatic amine (e.g. ethylamine) + HNO₂: gives an unstable aliphatic diazonium that decomposes immediately at any temperature, releasing N₂ as a steady stream of gas. Other products are messy: alkene, alcohol, halide.`,`1° aromatic amine (e.g. phenylamine): aromatic diazonium is STABLE in cold solution because the +N≡N group is stabilised by overlap with the ring π system. This stability is what makes coupling chemistry possible.`,`In short: cold HNO₂ + amine, watch for nitrogen gas. Steady gas → 1° aliphatic. No gas (cold), but a clear pale-yellow solution that effervesces only on warming → 1° aromatic.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`definition`,title:`Diazotisation`,body:`The conversion of a 1° aromatic amine to a diazonium salt using HNO₂ at <10 °C. This is the gateway to azo dyes (chapter 5).`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Aqueous bromine: 3 substitutions to give 2,4,6-tribromo-, room T, no catalyst, white ppt.`,`HNO₂ < 10 °C: forms benzenediazonium chloride C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻.`,`Same reagent > 10 °C: decomposes to phenol + N₂.`,`Aromatic diazonium is stable cold (ring-stabilised); aliphatic is not.`]}]},{id:`nc5`,title:`Diazonium Coupling & Dyes`,minutes:8,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`The benzenediazonium ion is a strongly electrophilic cation, but it is a comparatively gentle one — only electron-RICH aromatic rings (phenoxide ions, dialkylanilines, naphthols) react with it. The product is a coloured azo (–N=N–) compound and this is the chemistry behind a large fraction of synthetic dyes.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`AZO COUPLING · DYE FORMATION`,blocks:[{type:`steps`,items:[{body:`Diazotise phenylamine cold: C₆H₅NH₂ → C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻`,color:`primary`},{body:`Couple with alkaline phenol at low temperature`,color:`accent`},{body:`C₆H₅N₂⁺ + C₆H₅O⁻ → C₆H₅–N=N–C₆H₄–OH`,color:`pink`}]},{type:`panel`,title:`Product`,body:[`4-hydroxyazobenzene (orange dye)`,`extended π conjugation through –N=N– absorbs visible light`],color:`pink`},{type:`note`,text:`Changing the aromatic coupling component gives different azo-dye colours.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Diazotise cold, then couple — two steps to a dye`},{type:`h`,text:`Step 1: diazotise (chapter 4 recap)`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Phenylamine + NaNO₂ + HCl, in an ice bath (< 10 °C) → C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻.`,`Keep this solution COLD throughout — warming destroys the diazonium and you lose the synthesis.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Step 2: couple with phenol in alkali`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Mix the cold diazonium solution with phenol that has been DISSOLVED IN NaOH(aq) (so phenol is present as the more reactive phenoxide, C₆H₅O⁻).`,`Equation: C₆H₅N₂⁺ + C₆H₅O⁻ → 4-hydroxyazobenzene  (HO–C₆H₄–N=N–C₆H₅).`,`Observation: an immediate orange precipitate forms.`,`Why phenoxide and not phenol? –O⁻ is a stronger ring activator than –OH. The diazonium is a weak electrophile and needs all the help it can get.`,`Why position 4 (para)? Phenoxide directs ortho/para; the bulky diazonium prefers para to avoid steric clash with –O⁻.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Why is it coloured? — a quick chromophore picture`},{type:`ul`,items:[`The –N=N– bond joins two aromatic rings into one extended π system. The system has many close-spaced π and π* energy levels.`,`Some of the π → π* transitions now correspond to wavelengths in the VISIBLE range (rather than UV, as for benzene alone).`,`The molecule absorbs visible light at one wavelength and we see the COMPLEMENTARY colour. e.g. absorbs blue → looks orange.`,`Adding electron-donating or electron-withdrawing substituents to either ring shifts the absorption — that is how the same coupling reaction generates dozens of distinct dye colours.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Important industrial azo dyes`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Methyl orange: a sulfonate-bearing azo dye, used as a pH indicator (red below pH 3.1, yellow above pH 4.4).`,`Sunset yellow FCF (E110): food colouring.`,`Para red: textile dye, the first synthetic azo dye, made on factory scale from 1875.`,`Many azo dyes have been replaced or banned because of toxicology concerns (some metabolise to carcinogenic aromatic amines) — but the chemistry is still the foundation of the dye industry.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Coupling — when does it work?`,body:`Diazonium ion is a WEAK electrophile. It only attacks rings activated by strong donors (–O⁻, –NR₂, –OH). Plain benzene does not react. The coupling reaction will fail unless the partner ring is electron-rich.`},{type:`h`,text:`Other diazonium reactions (overview)`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Hydrolysis (warming in water): → phenol + N₂↑ + HCl. (covered in chapter 4)`,`These together make diazonium salts the most versatile aromatic intermediate in organic synthesis: from one 1° aromatic amine you can reach phenols, halides, nitriles, and dyes.`]},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Cold diazonium + alkaline phenol → orange azo dye.`,`Coupling needs an activated ring (phenoxide, dialkylaniline).`,`Colour comes from extended π system across –N=N–.`,`Diazonium chemistry connects amines to phenols, dyes, and many other aromatics.`]}]},{id:`nc6`,title:`Amides — Structure & Formation`,minutes:7,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`An amide has the general formula R–CO–NHR'. The nitrogen is directly attached to the carbonyl carbon. This single structural feature has dramatic consequences: amides are NOT basic (unlike amines), they have very high boiling points (because of strong N–H hydrogen bonding), and they form the linkage that runs through every protein in every living organism.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`AMIDES · R–CO–NH₂`,blocks:[{type:`panel`,title:`STRUCTURE`,body:[`R–C(=O)–NH₂`,`N lone pair delocalises into C=O`,`1° RCONH₂ · 2° RCONHR′ · 3° RCONR′R″`],color:`primary`},{type:`panel`,title:`FORMATION FROM ACYL CHLORIDE`,body:[`R–COCl + 2NH₃ → R–CONH₂ + NH₄Cl`,`R–COCl + 2R′NH₂ → R–CONHR′ + R′NH₃⁺Cl⁻`],color:`accent`},{type:`panel`,title:`PROPERTIES`,body:[`not basic: lone pair is delocalised into C=O`,`high m.p./b.p. from H-bonding`,`amide linkage is the peptide bond in proteins`],color:`pink`}]},caption:`Amide structure, formation, and key properties`},{type:`h`,text:`Classification by N substitution`},{type:`ul`,items:[`1° amide: R–CO–NH₂ (two H on N).`,`2° amide: R–CO–NH–R' (one H on N, one C). N-substituted.`,`3° amide: R–CO–NR'R'' (no H on N).`,`Naming for 1°: replace –oic acid with –amide. Ethanoic → ethanamide (CH₃CONH₂).`,`For 2°/3°: prefix groups attached to N with "N-". e.g. CH₃CONHCH₃ = N-methylethanamide.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Formation — the acyl chloride route`},{type:`ul`,items:[`The standard preparation in the syllabus: react an acyl chloride with ammonia or a primary amine.`,`With NH₃: R–COCl + 2NH₃ → R–CONH₂ + NH₄Cl. The first NH₃ is the nucleophile; the second mops up HCl as NH₄Cl. White solid product (1° amide).`,`With R'NH₂: R–COCl + 2R'NH₂ → R–CONHR' + R'NH₃⁺Cl⁻. Forms an N-substituted (2°) amide. The second equivalent of amine acts as a base.`,`Conditions: room temperature, often in dry ether or DCM. The reactions are vigorous because acyl chlorides are highly reactive (revisit OC-18, chapter 8).`,`Less commonly: heating an ammonium salt — CH₃COO⁻NH₄⁺ → CH₃CONH₂ + H₂O. Industrial but slow and impractical at lab scale.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Why amides are NOT basic`},{type:`ul`,items:[`In an amine, the N lone pair is freely available to bond H⁺ → basic.`,`In an amide, the N is bonded to the carbonyl C. The N lone pair delocalises INTO the C=O. The effective electron density on N is reduced.`,`Pictorially: the N–C bond is partially double; the C=O is partially single. The lone pair is shared with C and not free for H⁺.`,`Consequence: amides have pKb around 15–17 (essentially neutral), not 3–9 like amines. They will not turn red litmus blue. They will not protonate with dilute HCl in a useful way.`,`A common exam check: "Why is propan-1-amine basic but propanamide is not?" — answer: amide N lone pair is delocalised into the C=O.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Physical properties`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Most amides are solids at RT (even small ones — methanamide is a liquid, but ethanamide melts at 81 °C and boils at 222 °C).`,`High m.p./b.p. compared to alcohols of similar Mr: each amide has TWO N-H bonds AND a strong C=O acceptor — extensive 3D hydrogen bonding network.`,`Soluble in water for small amides (H-bond donors and acceptors); solubility falls off with chain length.`,`Cyclic amides (lactams) and the polyamides we will meet in OC-20 are essentially long chains of amide groups joined together.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`definition`,title:`Peptide bond`,body:`A peptide bond is just an amide bond, but the term is used when it joins two amino acid residues. Same chemistry, same restricted N rotation, same delocalisation. We will pick this up in chapter 10.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Amide = R–CO–N. Best made from R-COCl + amine/NH₃.`,`NOT basic — N lone pair delocalised into C=O.`,`High m.p./b.p. — strong intermolecular H-bonding.`,`Same chemistry as the peptide bond in proteins.`]}]},{id:`nc7`,title:`Hydrolysis of Amides`,minutes:7,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Amides are surprisingly stable — they need vigorous acid OR base + heat to hydrolyse. This stability is exactly what proteins require: a peptide chain must not fall apart in your bloodstream. But under harsh conditions the C–N bond does break, and the products tell you what was on either side of the original bond.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`HYDROLYSIS OF AMIDES`,blocks:[{type:`compare`,left:{title:`ACID HYDROLYSIS`,color:`primary`,body:[`RCONH₂ + H₂O + HCl → RCOOH + NH₄Cl`,`dilute HCl, reflux`,`acid + ammonium salt`]},right:{title:`BASE HYDROLYSIS`,color:`accent`,body:[`RCONH₂ + NaOH → RCOO⁻Na⁺ + NH₃↑`,`NaOH(aq), reflux`,`carboxylate salt + NH₃`]}},{type:`note`,text:`NH₃ evolved on warming an amide with NaOH turns damp red litmus blue.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Acid (left) and base (right) hydrolysis — different products`},{type:`h`,text:`Acid hydrolysis`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Equation: R–CONH₂ + H₂O + HCl → R–COOH + NH₄Cl.`,`Conditions: dilute HCl (or dilute H₂SO₄), reflux for 30–60 min.`,`Products: a carboxylic acid + an ammonium salt. Note the HCl ends up in the NH₄⁺Cl⁻ salt.`,`For an N-substituted amide: R–CONHR' + H₂O + HCl → R–COOH + R'NH₃⁺Cl⁻ (the amine is recovered as its ammonium salt).`]},{type:`h`,text:`Base hydrolysis`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Equation: R–CONH₂ + NaOH → R–COO⁻Na⁺ + NH₃↑.`,`Conditions: NaOH(aq), reflux for 30–60 min.`,`Products: a carboxylate SALT (not the free acid — NaOH consumes any acid that forms) + ammonia gas, which can be detected by its smell or by turning damp red litmus paper blue.`,`For N-substituted: R–CONHR' + NaOH → R–COO⁻Na⁺ + R'NH₂ (the free amine, not the salt — NaOH ensures the basic form).`]},{type:`h`,text:`Diagnostic value`},{type:`ul`,items:[`NH₃ evolution on heating with NaOH(aq) is a strong test for an AMIDE. Compare: amides give NH₃ on heating with alkali, but amines do not — amines simply remain as R-NH₂ (already a free amine, it is not "released" by NaOH).`,`However, ammonium salts of organic acids (e.g. CH₃COO⁻NH₄⁺) will also give off NH₃ with NaOH. To distinguish: a true amide is neutral (no smell of vinegar / no acid character on its own).`]},{type:`h`,text:`Why amide hydrolysis is slow`},{type:`ul`,items:[`The C of the amide is much less electrophilic than the C of an ester or acyl chloride — because the N pushes electron density onto C via the same delocalisation that explains non-basicity.`,`Also, the leaving group from the tetrahedral intermediate is RNH⁻ or NH₂⁻ — very poor leaving groups, requiring acid (to protonate to RNH₂ or NH₃, which leave better) or vigorous base + heat to drive the reaction.`,`Practical consequence in the body: peptide bonds in proteins survive blood pH and 37 °C unaided — but specific protease enzymes accelerate hydrolysis at the right places, by ~10⁹ fold.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Acid vs base hydrolysis — predict the products`,body:`Acid: amine emerges as the salt R-NH₃⁺. Base: amine emerges as the free amine R-NH₂. The acid traps the basic product; the base traps the acidic product. This pattern repeats for esters too.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Acid: R-CONH₂ + HCl → R-COOH + NH₄Cl.`,`Base: R-CONH₂ + NaOH → R-COO⁻Na⁺ + NH₃↑.`,`Both need REFLUX — amide is the most stable acyl derivative.`,`NH₃ evolution with NaOH = amide test.`]}]},{id:`nc8`,title:`Amino Acids`,minutes:7,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`An α-amino acid has both an amino group (–NH₂) and a carboxylic acid group (–COOH) attached to the same carbon, called the α-carbon. The general formula is H₂N–CHR–COOH, where R is the side chain. There are 20 standard α-amino acids; their side chains R differ in size, polarity, charge, and reactivity, and these differences determine the structure and function of every protein.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`α-AMINO ACID · H₂N–CHR–COOH`,blocks:[{type:`panel`,title:`GENERAL STRUCTURE`,body:[`H₂N–C(H)(R)–COOH`,`NH₂ = basic group; COOH = acidic group`,`R = side chain; α-carbon usually chiral`],color:`primary`},{type:`table`,headers:[`Amino acid`,`R group`],rows:[[`glycine`,`H`],[`alanine`,`CH₃`],[`serine`,`CH₂OH`],[`cysteine`,`CH₂SH`],[`valine`,`CH(CH₃)₂`]]},{type:`note`,text:`Glycine is the exception: R = H, so the α-carbon is not chiral.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`The α-amino acid skeleton with R = side chain`},{type:`h`,text:`The α-carbon is chiral (with one exception)`},{type:`ul`,items:[`For 19 of the 20 standard amino acids, the α-C has FOUR different groups attached: H, NH₂, COOH, R. So it is a chiral centre, and the molecule has two enantiomers (mirror images) called L and D.`,`Glycine (R = H) is the exception: now the α-C has TWO H atoms, so it is not chiral.`,`Biology uses ONLY L-amino acids in proteins. The reasons go back to the chemistry of the genetic code and to early biochemistry; the consequence for us is that proteins have a definite handedness.`,`Optical rotation: L-amino acids rotate plane-polarised light. Some happen to be levorotatory (–), some dextrorotatory (+); "L" refers to a stereochemical label, not the sign.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Side chains R define the amino acid`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Non-polar (hydrophobic): glycine (R=H), alanine (CH₃), valine ((CH₃)₂CH-), leucine, isoleucine, methionine, phenylalanine. These cluster in protein interiors away from water.`,`Polar uncharged: serine (CH₂OH), threonine, asparagine, glutamine, cysteine (CH₂SH). They form hydrogen bonds.`,`Acidic side chains: aspartate (CH₂COOH), glutamate (CH₂CH₂COOH). At physiological pH these are deprotonated, –COO⁻.`,`Basic side chains: lysine, arginine, histidine. These are protonated at physiological pH, –NH₃⁺ etc.`,`Aromatic: phenylalanine, tyrosine, tryptophan.`,`Special: proline (cyclic, the N is in a ring), cysteine (forms disulfide bridges, S–S, between residues).`]},{type:`h`,text:`Naming and abbreviations`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Each amino acid has a common name (glycine, alanine), a 3-letter abbreviation (Gly, Ala), and a single-letter code (G, A).`,`IUPAC names exist (glycine = aminoethanoic acid; alanine = 2-aminopropanoic acid) but are rarely used in practice.`,`The 3-letter and 1-letter codes are how protein sequences are written. e.g. "Met-Lys-Val..." or "MKV..." for the start of a protein.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Why amino acids are unusual physically`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Amino acids in pure form melt at very high temperatures (often > 200 °C, with decomposition).`,`They are crystalline solids, water-soluble, but barely soluble in non-polar solvents.`,`They have very low vapour pressure — they do not behave like volatile organic molecules.`,`These properties resemble those of ionic salts, NOT typical organics. The reason is that in the solid and in water they exist as ZWITTERIONS — chapter 9.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`definition`,title:`α-amino acid`,body:`An organic compound containing both a carboxylic acid (–COOH) and an amino group (–NH₂) on adjacent carbons. The α-position is the carbon next to the –COOH group.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`General formula: H₂N–CHR–COOH. The R defines which amino acid.`,`α-C is chiral except in glycine; biology uses L only.`,`20 standard amino acids; classified by side chain.`,`Solid m.p. > 200 °C, water-soluble, low volatility — ionic-like behaviour.`]}]},{id:`nc9`,title:`Zwitterions & Acid-Base Behaviour`,minutes:9,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`A zwitterion is a single molecule that carries a positive charge AND a negative charge in different places — net zero charge but full ionic separation. Amino acids are the textbook example. Their acid (–COOH) and base (–NH₂) groups are on the same molecule, and the acid protonates the base internally. This explains the high melting points and water solubility we noted in chapter 8 and gives amino acids a pH-dependent behaviour you must master.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`ZWITTERION & pH-DEPENDENT CHARGE`,blocks:[{type:`cards`,cards:[{title:`LOW pH`,color:`pink`,body:[`H₃N⁺–CHR–COOH`,`net charge +1`,`cation`]},{title:`AT pI`,color:`primary`,body:[`H₃N⁺–CHR–COO⁻`,`net charge 0`,`zwitterion`]},{title:`HIGH pH`,color:`green`,body:[`H₂N–CHR–COO⁻`,`net charge −1`,`anion`]}]},{type:`panel`,title:`Zwitterion properties`,body:[`internally ionic despite net zero charge`,`high melting point (>200 °C)`,`water-soluble and non-volatile`],color:`accent`},{type:`note`,text:`pI = pH at which the amino acid has zero net charge.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Three forms of an amino acid as pH changes`},{type:`h`,text:`Why a zwitterion forms`},{type:`ul`,items:[`In a typical amino acid, –COOH has pKa ≈ 2.3 and the –NH₃⁺ on the α-N has pKa ≈ 9.6. (For glycine specifically: pKa₁ = 2.34, pKa₂ = 9.60.)`,`At neutral pH (7), the –COOH is well above its pKa — fully deprotonated to –COO⁻.`,`At pH 7, the –NH₃⁺ form is well below its pKa — protonated to –NH₃⁺.`,`The MOST stable form at neutral pH is therefore both ionised: H₃N⁺–CHR–COO⁻. This is the zwitterion.`,`There is no covalent change — just intramolecular proton transfer from –COOH to –NH₂.`]},{type:`h`,text:`pH-dependent behaviour`},{type:`ul`,items:[`At LOW pH (strongly acidic, pH ≪ pKa₁): both groups protonated. Form: H₃N⁺–CHR–COOH. Net charge +1 (cation).`,`At INTERMEDIATE pH around pI: zwitterion. H₃N⁺–CHR–COO⁻. Net charge 0.`,`At HIGH pH (strongly basic, pH ≫ pKa₂): both groups deprotonated. Form: H₂N–CHR–COO⁻. Net charge −1 (anion).`,`In an electric field, the cation (low pH) migrates to the cathode; the anion (high pH) migrates to the anode; the zwitterion at pI does not migrate. This is the principle behind electrophoresis.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Isoelectric point (pI)`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Definition: the pH at which the amino acid carries NO net charge (the zwitterion form predominates).`,`For amino acids with neutral side chains: pI = (pKa₁ + pKa₂) / 2. e.g. glycine pI = (2.34 + 9.60)/2 = 5.97.`,`For acidic side chains (Asp, Glu): pI is LOWER (around 3) because there are two acidic groups.`,`For basic side chains (Lys, Arg, His): pI is HIGHER (around 9–11) because there are two basic groups.`,`Practical use: at its pI, an amino acid is least soluble in water (no net charge to attract water) — separation by isoelectric precipitation.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Titration curve`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`AMINO-ACID TITRATION · GLYCINE + NaOH`,blocks:[{type:`raw`,height:190,primitives:[{p:`line`,x1:45,y1:170,x2:300,y2:170,color:`text`},{p:`line`,x1:45,y1:20,x2:45,y2:170,color:`text`},{p:`path`,d:`M50 150 Q80 148 110 142 Q130 122 150 112 Q170 103 190 80 Q215 50 245 34 Q270 28 295 26`,color:`primary`,width:2},{p:`line`,x1:88,y1:150,x2:88,y2:118,color:`pink`,dash:!0},{p:`text`,x:88,y:110,text:`pKa₁ ≈ 2.34`,color:`pink`,size:7,bold:!0},{p:`line`,x1:148,y1:112,x2:148,y2:84,color:`primary`,dash:!0},{p:`text`,x:148,y:76,text:`pI ≈ 5.97`,color:`primary`,size:7,bold:!0},{p:`line`,x1:210,y1:60,x2:210,y2:34,color:`green`,dash:!0},{p:`text`,x:210,y:26,text:`pKa₂ ≈ 9.60`,color:`green`,size:7,bold:!0},{p:`text`,x:170,y:185,text:`volume of NaOH added →`,color:`dim`,size:8},{p:`text`,x:20,y:95,text:`pH`,color:`dim`,size:8}]},{type:`note`,text:`Two pKa values reflect two ionisable groups: COOH first, then NH₃⁺.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Two pKa values give two buffer plateaus`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Start at low pH with the cation. Add NaOH gradually.`,`First buffer plateau around pH = pKa₁: removing the proton from –COOH.`,`Sharp jump through pI: the zwitterion dominates.`,`Second buffer plateau around pH = pKa₂: removing the proton from –NH₃⁺.`,`Continue: end with the anion form.`,`For amino acids with ionisable side chains, expect a third buffer plateau at the side-chain pKa.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Why crystals are high-melting`},{type:`ul`,items:[`In the solid, amino acids pack as zwitterions. The crystal is held together by strong electrostatic attractions between –NH₃⁺ and –COO⁻ on neighbouring molecules.`,`These ionic interactions are much stronger than the dipole-dipole or H-bond interactions in non-zwitterionic organic solids.`,`Consequence: m.p. > 200 °C (often with decomposition before melting), low volatility, low solubility in non-polar solvents but high solubility in water.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Predict the form: compare pH to pI`,body:`pH ≪ pI → cation (positive). pH = pI → zwitterion (neutral). pH ≫ pI → anion (negative). This rule is enough to answer "What is the predominant form of glycine at pH 4?" — answer: pH 4 < pI 5.97, so the cation H₃N⁺–CH₂–COOH dominates (more precisely, a mix of cation and zwitterion).`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Zwitterion: H₃N⁺–CHR–COO⁻, full ⁺/⁻ separation, net zero.`,`Forms because pKa₁ (COOH) ≈ 2 and pKa₂ (NH₃⁺) ≈ 10.`,`pI = (pKa₁ + pKa₂)/2 for neutral side chains.`,`pH < pI → cation; pH > pI → anion. Useful in electrophoresis.`]}]},{id:`nc10`,title:`Peptides & Proteins`,minutes:7,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`When the –COOH of one amino acid joins the –NH₂ of another, with loss of water, the product is a dipeptide. Repeat the process and you build a polypeptide — an unbranched chain of amino acids joined head-to-tail by amide (peptide) bonds. Proteins are simply long polypeptides folded into specific 3D shapes.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`PEPTIDE BOND · AN AMIDE LINK BETWEEN AMINO ACIDS`,blocks:[{type:`equation`,text:`H₂N–CHR–COOH + H₂N–CHR′–COOH`},{type:`arrow`,text:`↓ condensation (−H₂O)`},{type:`equation`,text:`H₂N–CHR–CO–NH–CHR′–COOH`,big:!0},{type:`panel`,title:`–CO–NH– = peptide bond`,body:[`repeat condensation → polypeptide → protein`,`R-group sequence defines primary structure`,`C=O···H–N hydrogen bonding supports α-helices/β-sheets`],color:`accent`},{type:`note`,text:`Hydrolysis reverses peptide formation and liberates amino acids.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Two amino acids → dipeptide + H₂O`},{type:`h`,text:`Forming the peptide bond`},{type:`ul`,items:[`General reaction: H₂N–CHR–COOH + H₂N–CHR'–COOH → H₂N–CHR–CO–NH–CHR'–COOH + H₂O.`,`The –COOH of one residue and the –NH₂ of the next condense; one H₂O is released per bond formed.`,`This is just amide formation. In the lab it requires activation (e.g. via acyl chloride) — direct condensation is too slow at room temperature. In cells, ribosomes catalyse it with enzymes and ATP.`,`A dipeptide has ONE peptide bond. A tripeptide has two peptide bonds. A polypeptide of n residues has (n − 1) peptide bonds.`,`Convention: the chain has an N-terminus (free –NH₂) on the LEFT and a C-terminus (free –COOH) on the RIGHT. The order matters — Gly-Ala is different from Ala-Gly.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Levels of protein structure (overview)`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Primary structure: the sequence of amino acid residues, written from N-terminus to C-terminus. Encoded by DNA via the genetic code.`,`Secondary structure: regular folding patterns formed by H-bonds between –C=O and N–H of the backbone. The two main motifs are the α-helix (right-handed coil) and the β-pleated sheet (extended strands H-bonded side-by-side).`,`Tertiary structure: the overall 3D fold of one polypeptide, stabilised by side-chain interactions — H-bonds, ionic bridges (–COO⁻···⁺H₃N–), van der Waals, hydrophobic clustering, and –S–S– disulfide links between cysteines.`,`Quaternary structure: assembly of two or more separate polypeptide subunits into one functional protein (haemoglobin has 4 subunits, for instance).`]},{type:`h`,text:`Why peptide bonds are special`},{type:`ul`,items:[`The peptide bond is partially double in character — the N lone pair delocalises into the C=O. So the bond is shorter than a typical C–N single bond and rotation around it is restricted.`,`Six atoms — Cα, C=O, N, H, Cα' (and the H on N, the O on C) — lie in approximately one plane.`,`This planarity constrains protein folding: only the backbone bonds either side of the peptide unit can rotate (the φ and ψ angles).`,`It also makes peptide bonds resistant to hydrolysis under physiological conditions — exactly what is needed to keep proteins intact in the body.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`definition`,title:`Peptide vs protein`,body:`"Peptide" is a general term for a chain of amino acids joined by peptide bonds. "Polypeptide" usually means a longer chain (often >20 residues). "Protein" usually means a polypeptide that has folded into its functional 3D shape, often with non-amino-acid co-factors (haem, metal ions, glycans) attached. There is no strict cut-off in length — usage is conventional.`},{type:`h`,text:`Drawing dipeptides — exam tip`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Question may ask: "Draw the structure of the dipeptide Gly-Ala." Always read N-terminus first.`,`Step 1: write glycine on the left with its –NH₂ free. H₂N–CH₂–CO–.`,`Step 2: write alanine on the right with its –COOH free. –NH–CH(CH₃)–COOH.`,`Step 3: join them at the peptide bond shown by –CO–NH–.`,`Final: H₂N–CH₂–CO–NH–CH(CH₃)–COOH.`,`For "Ala-Gly" the order is reversed: H₂N–CH(CH₃)–CO–NH–CH₂–COOH.`]},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Peptide bond = amide bond between α-amino acids; loses H₂O.`,`Direction: N-terminus → C-terminus; the order matters.`,`Primary structure = sequence; higher orders are folding patterns.`,`Restricted rotation at the peptide bond shapes the whole protein.`]}]},{id:`nc11`,title:`Hydrolysis & Analysis of Proteins`,minutes:7,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`To find out which amino acids make up a protein, we hydrolyse it back to the free residues and identify them by chromatography or electrophoresis. The hydrolysis is the reverse of peptide-bond formation — the conditions are exactly those for amide hydrolysis from chapter 7, scaled up to a polymer. The analysis ties this topic to phase equilibria (AC-22).`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`HYDROLYSIS & ANALYSIS OF PROTEINS`,blocks:[{type:`panel`,title:`ACID HYDROLYSIS`,body:[`6 M HCl, reflux ~24 h`,`free amino-acid mixture`,`can destroy tryptophan and deamidate Asn/Gln`],color:`primary`},{type:`panel`,title:`BASE HYDROLYSIS`,body:[`conc. NaOH, reflux`,`free amino acids`,`may cause racemisation / side-chain damage`],color:`accent`},{type:`panel`,title:`ENZYMATIC HYDROLYSIS`,body:[`specific proteases such as trypsin/chymotrypsin`,`gentle and selective`,`useful for sequence mapping`],color:`pink`},{type:`note`,text:`Separate hydrolysate by TLC/electrophoresis; ninhydrin reveals amino-acid spots.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Three ways to hydrolyse a protein, then visualise spots`},{type:`h`,text:`Acid hydrolysis`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Conditions: ~6 M HCl, sealed tube, 110 °C, 24 hours. (Or microwave-assisted, faster.)`,`Result: every peptide bond is broken; the protein is reduced to a mixture of free amino acids in their cation form (low pH).`,`After hydrolysis, neutralise to pI for analysis.`,`Drawback: tryptophan is destroyed. Asparagine and glutamine lose their side-chain –NH₂ to become aspartate and glutamate. So acid hydrolysis miscounts these four — they need separate determination.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Base hydrolysis`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Conditions: concentrated NaOH or KOH, reflux.`,`Drawback: causes RACEMISATION (L → D/L mix) at the α-carbon and destroys some side chains (Ser, Thr, Cys, Arg).`,`Used mainly when tryptophan content is needed — Trp survives base hydrolysis.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Enzymatic hydrolysis`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Specific protease enzymes cut at specific peptide bonds.`,`Trypsin: cleaves on the C-terminal side of lysine (K) and arginine (R).`,`Chymotrypsin: cleaves on the C-terminal side of large hydrophobic residues (F, W, Y).`,`Mild conditions (37 °C, neutral pH) — used to map sequences.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Separation and detection`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Paper chromatography or thin-layer chromatography (TLC): different amino acids have different Rf values in a chosen solvent. (Recall AC-22.)`,`Electrophoresis: at a chosen pH, amino acids with different pI values acquire different charges and migrate at different rates in an electric field.`,`Visualisation with NINHYDRIN: spray the chromatogram or gel with ninhydrin solution and warm. Most amino acids give a violet/purple spot. Proline gives a YELLOW spot (a known exception — proline's α-N is in a ring and reacts differently).`,`Modern variants include HPLC with UV/fluorescence detection and mass spectrometry — touched on in AC-22.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Worked sequencing example`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Suppose a tetrapeptide P gives, after total acid hydrolysis, equal amounts of Gly, Ala, Lys, Phe.`,`Trypsin cleaves P after Lys, giving fragments: F1 = (3 residues, contains Lys at C-end) and F2 = (1 residue).`,`Chymotrypsin cleaves P after Phe, giving fragments: G1 = (2 residues, contains Phe at C-end) and G2 = (2 residues).`,`Combining the two patterns: only one sequence — Ala-Phe-Gly-Lys — fits both partial digests. (Detail: trypsin gives Ala-Phe-Gly-Lys / nothing... actually trypsin would cut after Lys but Lys is C-terminal so would leave it intact in this case; chymotrypsin would cut after Phe to give Ala-Phe / Gly-Lys.)`,`This kind of partial-digest jigsaw is the principle behind early protein sequencing methods (Sanger, Edman). Modern methods use mass spec.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Hydrolysis = the reverse of formation`,body:`Synthesising peptides loses H₂O at each bond. Hydrolysis ADDS H₂O at each bond. The number of moles of water released or consumed equals the number of peptide bonds, which is (n − 1) for an n-residue peptide.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Acid hydrolysis: 6 M HCl, reflux 24 h — destroys Trp.`,`Base hydrolysis: NaOH, reflux — racemises α-C; spares Trp.`,`Enzymatic: specific cuts, used for sequencing.`,`Detect free amino acids with ninhydrin (purple; proline yellow).`,`Separation by chromatography or electrophoresis (AC-22).`]}]},{id:`nc12`,title:`Identification & Synthesis Strategy`,minutes:6,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`This closing chapter consolidates the chemistry of OC-19. Two skills to lock in: (1) given an unknown nitrogen compound, deduce what it is from a sequence of test results; (2) given a starting material, plan a multi-step synthesis to a target nitrogen compound. Both are routine exam tasks and reward rehearsed pattern recognition.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`IDENTIFYING NITROGEN COMPOUNDS · DECISION GUIDE`,blocks:[{type:`table`,headers:[`Test / observation`,`Likely class`],rows:[[`HNO₂ <10 °C → steady N₂`,`1° aliphatic amine`],[`HNO₂ <10 °C → stable diazonium solution`,`1° aromatic amine`],[`HNO₂ → yellow N-nitroso oil`,`2° amine`],[`NaOH + heat → NH₃`,`amide`],[`ninhydrin → purple`,`amino acid`],[`Br₂(aq) → white 2,4,6-tribromo ppt`,`1° aromatic amine`]]},{type:`note`,text:`Warm an aromatic diazonium salt → phenol + N₂.`,emphasis:!0},{type:`note`,text:`Use a sequence of independent observations; do not identify an unknown from one test alone.`}]},caption:`Test sequence and decision tree for unknowns`},{type:`h`,text:`Identification — the test sequence to memorise`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Step 1: Is it an AMINE? Add HCl. An amine dissolves and forms an ionic salt. (Amides, nitriles, amino acids do not behave as simple bases here.)`,`Step 2: Is it a 1°/2°/3° amine? Add HNO₂ at < 10 °C. (a) Steady N₂ gas at all temperatures → 1° aliphatic amine. (b) Stable cold solution; warming gives N₂ + phenol-like product → 1° aromatic amine (phenylamine). (c) Yellow oily layer (N-nitroso compound) → 2° amine. (d) No obvious reaction → 3° amine (or amide).`,`Step 3: Aromatic 1° amine? Confirm with Br₂(aq) at RT — instant white precipitate (2,4,6-tribromophenylamine).`,`Step 4: Is it an AMIDE? Heat with NaOH(aq). Damp red litmus paper goes blue at the mouth of the tube → NH₃ → amide.`,`Step 5: Is it an AMINO ACID? Spray with NINHYDRIN, warm. Purple/violet spot → amino acid (yellow → proline, exception).`]},{type:`h`,text:`Synthesis strategy — common chains`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Halogenoalkane → 1° amine (chain extension): R–X → R–CN (KCN/EtOH, reflux) → R–CH₂–NH₂ (LiAlH₄/dry ether or H₂/Ni).`,`Benzene → phenylamine: C₆H₆ → C₆H₅NO₂ (HNO₃/H₂SO₄, 50 °C) → C₆H₅NH₂ (Sn/conc. HCl, then NaOH).`,`Phenylamine → phenol: C₆H₅NH₂ → C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ (NaNO₂/HCl, < 10 °C) → C₆H₅OH (warm in water).`,`Phenylamine → azo dye: diazotise (cold) → couple with phenoxide.`,`Carboxylic acid → 1° amide: R–COOH → R–COCl (SOCl₂) → R–CONH₂ (NH₃).`,`1° amide → carboxylic acid (or amine): R–CONH₂ + NaOH/heat → R–COO⁻Na⁺ + NH₃. Acidify to recover R–COOH.`,`Polypeptide → free amino acids: 6 M HCl, reflux 24 h.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Common exam errors to avoid`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Saying "amides are basic". They are NOT — N lone pair delocalised into C=O.`,`Forgetting the temperature requirement for diazotisation (< 10 °C). Above this you get phenol, not the salt.`,`Reporting "phenylamine + HNO₂ gives N₂". No — at low T it gives the diazonium salt; only on warming does N₂ evolve.`,`Confusing the order of reagents in coupling. Phenol + NaOH FIRST (to make phenoxide), THEN add cold diazonium.`,`Drawing the zwitterion at pH 1 or pH 13 — at those extremes the cation/anion forms dominate, not the zwitterion.`,`Quoting "alkylamines stronger because they are bigger" — molecular size is not the reason; the +I effect is.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Three ideas tie this whole topic together`,body:`(1) The N lone pair drives basicity, nucleophilic attack, and dye coupling. (2) Delocalisation of the N lone pair (into a C=O or into a benzene ring) reduces basicity but adds new chemistry. (3) Acid/base behaviour at the same molecule (zwitterion) explains the unusual physical properties of amino acids and gives proteins their charge.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Test sequence: HCl → HNO₂ → Br₂ → NaOH/heat → ninhydrin.`,`Chain extension via nitrile is the most common synthesis manoeuvre.`,`The N lone pair is the key — its availability determines reactivity.`,`Practice multi-step plans on past papers; the same chains repeat.`]}]}],quizzes:[{q:`Which compound is a primary amine?`,opts:[`CH₃NHCH₃`,`(CH₃)₃N`,`CH₃CH₂NH₂`,`(CH₃)₄N⁺Cl⁻`],ans:2,hint:`Primary = N has TWO H atoms and ONE alkyl/aryl group.`},{q:`The IUPAC name for CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂ is:`,opts:[`Propanal`,`Propanamine (or propylamine)`,`Propan-1-ol`,`Propanenitrile`],ans:1,hint:`3 C with –NH₂ on C1.`},{q:`Reduction of CH₃CN with LiAlH₄/dry ether gives:`,opts:[`CH₃CHO`,`CH₃CH₂NH₂`,`CH₃COOH`,`CH₃NH₂`],ans:1,hint:`Nitrile reduction extends chain by 1 C, giving 1° amine.`},{q:`Phenylamine is best prepared from:`,opts:[`Benzene + HCl`,`Nitrobenzene + Sn/conc. HCl, then NaOH`,`Bromobenzene + KCN`,`Benzoic acid + NaOH`],ans:1,hint:`Reduction of –NO₂ to –NH₂ in two stages.`},{q:`Order of basicity (strongest first):`,opts:[`Phenylamine > NH₃ > ethylamine`,`Ethylamine > NH₃ > phenylamine`,`NH₃ > ethylamine > phenylamine`,`All equal`],ans:1,hint:`Alkyl +I effect > NH₃ > aryl delocalisation.`},{q:`Phenylamine is a weaker base than NH₃ because:`,opts:[`It is bigger`,`The N lone pair is delocalised into the benzene ring`,`It is insoluble`,`It has no lone pair`],ans:1,hint:`Lone pair tied up in aromatic π system → less available.`},{q:`Ethylamine reacts with HCl(aq) to form:`,opts:[`No reaction`,`CH₃CH₂NH₃⁺Cl⁻ (ionic salt)`,`Chloroethane + NH₃`,`Ethanal + NH₄Cl`],ans:1,hint:`Amine + acid → ammonium salt.`},{q:`Phenylamine + Br₂(aq) at room temperature gives:`,opts:[`No reaction`,`Bromobenzene + NH₃`,`Mono-bromo-phenylamine`,`2,4,6-tribromophenylamine (white ppt)`],ans:3,hint:`NH₂ activates ring strongly — three Br atoms substitute.`},{q:`Bromination of phenylamine does NOT need a halogen carrier (FeBr₃) because:`,opts:[`Br₂ is too reactive`,`The –NH₂ group strongly activates the ring`,`It is a free-radical reaction`,`Br₂ dissolves in water`],ans:1,hint:`N lone pair pumps e⁻ into ring; ring is already activated.`},{q:`For phenylamine + HNO₂, the temperature must be:`,opts:[`Above 30 °C`,`At reflux`,`Below 10 °C (ice bath)`,`At room temperature`],ans:2,hint:`Above 10 °C the diazonium decomposes to phenol.`},{q:`The product of phenylamine + NaNO₂ + HCl at 5 °C is:`,opts:[`C₆H₅OH`,`C₆H₅NO₂`,`C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ (benzenediazonium chloride)`,`C₆H₅NH₃⁺Cl⁻`],ans:2,hint:`Diazotisation: NH₂ → N₂⁺.`},{q:`Warming benzenediazonium chloride in water gives:`,opts:[`Aniline + HCl`,`Phenol + N₂↑ + HCl`,`Benzene + N₂`,`Nitrobenzene`],ans:1,hint:`Hydrolysis releases N₂ — visible effervescence.`},{q:`For azo coupling with phenol, the phenol should be:`,opts:[`In dilute acid`,`In alkaline solution (as phenoxide)`,`Pure phenol`,`Heated to 100 °C`],ans:1,hint:`–O⁻ is a stronger ring activator than –OH.`},{q:`The orange product from coupling C₆H₅N₂⁺ with phenoxide is:`,opts:[`Phenyl ether`,`4-hydroxyazobenzene`,`Diphenylamine`,`Phenyl benzoate`],ans:1,hint:`Para-coupling of N=N onto the ring.`},{q:`Why are azo compounds coloured?`,opts:[`They contain nitrogen`,`They have extended conjugation through –N=N– absorbing in visible region`,`They are charged`,`They contain benzene rings`],ans:1,hint:`Long π-system → small HOMO–LUMO gap → visible absorption.`},{q:`Best preparation of ethanamide CH₃CONH₂ is:`,opts:[`Ethanoic acid + NH₃ at RT`,`Ethanoyl chloride + NH₃`,`Ethanal + NH₃`,`Ethene + NH₃`],ans:1,hint:`Acyl chloride + ammonia → 1° amide.`},{q:`Why are amides NOT basic?`,opts:[`No lone pair on N`,`Amide N is sp`,`N lone pair is delocalised into C=O`,`Amides are insoluble`],ans:2,hint:`Lone pair tied up in C=O conjugation.`},{q:`Acid hydrolysis of CH₃CONH₂ gives:`,opts:[`CH₃NH₂ + CO₂`,`CH₃COOH + NH₄Cl`,`CH₃CHO + NH₃`,`CH₃CN + H₂O`],ans:1,hint:`Amide + HCl/H₂O reflux → acid + ammonium salt.`},{q:`Base hydrolysis of CH₃CONH₂ gives:`,opts:[`CH₃COOH + NH₃`,`CH₃COO⁻Na⁺ + NH₃↑`,`CH₃NH₂ + CO₂`,`No reaction`],ans:1,hint:`NaOH consumes the acid; NH₃ released as gas.`},{q:`NH₃ evolution on heating with NaOH(aq) is a positive test for:`,opts:[`1° amine`,`1° amide or ammonium salt`,`Carboxylic acid`,`Ester`],ans:1,hint:`Both amides and ammonium salts release NH₃.`},{q:`The general formula of an α-amino acid is:`,opts:[`H₂N–R–OH`,`H₂N–CHR–COOH`,`R–CONH₂`,`R–N(R')–R''`],ans:1,hint:`–NH₂ and –COOH on the same C (α-carbon).`},{q:`Which amino acid has NO chiral α-carbon?`,opts:[`Alanine`,`Valine`,`Glycine`,`Serine`],ans:2,hint:`Glycine R = H, so two H on α-C → no chirality.`},{q:`A zwitterion is:`,opts:[`A neutral molecule with no charges`,`A molecule with both positive and negative charges, net zero`,`A molecule with two negative charges`,`An ionic salt`],ans:1,hint:`Internal proton transfer COOH → NH₂.`},{q:`At pH = pI for glycine, the predominant form is:`,opts:[`H₃N⁺–CH₂–COOH`,`H₃N⁺–CH₂–COO⁻ (zwitterion)`,`H₂N–CH₂–COO⁻`,`H₂N–CH₂–COOH`],ans:1,hint:`pI defines the pH where zwitterion dominates.`},{q:`For glycine pKa₁ = 2.34, pKa₂ = 9.60. Its pI is:`,opts:[`2.34`,`5.97`,`7.00`,`9.60`],ans:1,hint:`pI = (pKa₁ + pKa₂) / 2 for neutral side chains.`},{q:`At pH 12, glycine exists predominantly as:`,opts:[`Cation H₃N⁺-CH₂-COOH`,`Zwitterion`,`Anion H₂N-CH₂-COO⁻`,`Neutral H₂N-CH₂-COOH`],ans:2,hint:`pH well above pKa₂ (9.60) → both groups deprotonated.`},{q:`Why do amino acids have unusually high melting points?`,opts:[`They are aromatic`,`Strong covalent bonds`,`They exist as zwitterions held by ionic forces in the solid`,`They are polymers`],ans:2,hint:`Crystal lattice = zwitterions held by NH₃⁺···⁻OOC attractions.`},{q:`A peptide bond is:`,opts:[`A C-C bond`,`An ester bond`,`An amide bond between two amino acids`,`A disulfide bridge`],ans:2,hint:`Same chemistry as amide; just between α-amino acids.`},{q:`Forming a dipeptide from 2 amino acids releases:`,opts:[`NH₃`,`H₂O`,`CO₂`,`HCl`],ans:1,hint:`Condensation: COOH + NH₂ → amide + H₂O.`},{q:`Number of peptide bonds in a tripeptide is:`,opts:[`1`,`2`,`3`,`6`],ans:1,hint:`n residues → (n−1) peptide bonds.`},{q:`Acid hydrolysis of a protein produces:`,opts:[`Free amino acids in their cation form`,`Polypeptide fragments only`,`CO₂ + NH₃`,`No reaction`],ans:0,hint:`6 M HCl, reflux 24h → all bonds broken; cations dominate at low pH.`},{q:`Reagent that produces a violet/purple spot with most amino acids on a chromatogram:`,opts:[`Iodine`,`Bromine water`,`Ninhydrin`,`AgNO₃`],ans:2,hint:`Standard amino acid stain. Note: proline gives yellow.`}],multi:[],structured:[{title:`Preparation of amines`,marks:8,q:`Two preparations of primary amines are widely used.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Write equations and state conditions for the preparation of (i) ethylamine from a nitrile, and (ii) phenylamine from nitrobenzene.`,marks:4,ans:`(i) Ethylamine from a nitrile: CH₃CN + 4[H] → CH₃CH₂NH₂. Reagents: LiAlH₄ in dry ether at room temperature, OR H₂(g) with a Ni catalyst at high T,P. (ii) Phenylamine from nitrobenzene: C₆H₅NO₂ + 6[H] → C₆H₅NH₂ + 2H₂O. Reagents: Sn (or Fe) with concentrated HCl, reflux. The initial product is the soluble salt C₆H₅NH₃⁺Cl⁻; add NaOH(aq) to liberate the free amine, then steam-distil.`},{q:`(b) Explain why route (i) is described as a chain-extension synthesis. Illustrate by converting CH₃CH₂Br to a 3-carbon amine.`,marks:2,ans:`The nitrile route adds ONE carbon to the chain. The C of the cyanide group becomes part of the alkyl skeleton in the product. Example: CH₃CH₂Br (2 C) + KCN(alc, reflux) → CH₃CH₂CN (3 C) + KBr. Then CH₃CH₂CN + 4[H] (LiAlH₄/dry ether) → CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂ (3 C, propan-1-amine).`},{q:`(c) Direct reaction of bromoethane with NH₃ gives ethylamine, but the yield is low and the product is contaminated. Explain.`,marks:2,ans:`The first product CH₃CH₂NH₂ is itself a nucleophile. It competes with NH₃ for unreacted CH₃CH₂Br, giving a 2° amine (CH₃CH₂)₂NH; this in turn gives a 3° amine (CH₃CH₂)₃N; and a final reaction with bromoethane gives the quaternary ammonium salt (CH₃CH₂)₄N⁺Br⁻. The product is therefore a MIXTURE of 1°/2°/3°/4° amines, separable only with difficulty. Excess NH₃ pushes the balance towards the 1° product but does not eliminate the side reactions.`}]},{title:`Basicity argument`,marks:8,q:`The pKb values of methylamine, ammonia and phenylamine are 3.36, 4.75 and 9.42 respectively.`,parts:[{q:`(a) State which of the three is the strongest base and which is the weakest, and identify the relationship between pKb and base strength.`,marks:2,ans:`Strongest base = methylamine (smallest pKb = 3.36). Weakest base = phenylamine (largest pKb = 9.42). Smaller pKb means a more favourable equilibrium for accepting H⁺, hence a stronger base. (Equivalently, pKb = −log Kb, and a larger Kb means stronger basicity.)`},{q:`(b) Account for the pKb difference between methylamine and ammonia.`,marks:2,ans:`In methylamine, the alkyl group CH₃ exhibits a positive inductive effect (+I). It pushes electron density along the C–N bond and onto the nitrogen atom. As a result, the nitrogen lone pair has higher electron density and is more available to bond a proton. Methylamine therefore accepts H⁺ more readily than ammonia, and the conjugate acid CH₃NH₃⁺ is also stabilised by the +I effect of CH₃. Both factors give methylamine the smaller pKb.`},{q:`(c) Explain why phenylamine is a much weaker base than ammonia.`,marks:2,ans:`In phenylamine, the nitrogen lone pair occupies a p-type orbital that overlaps with the π-system of the benzene ring. The lone pair therefore delocalises INTO the ring, becoming spread over four positions (N, ortho, para). The electron density on N is reduced, and the lone pair is less available to bond a proton. Furthermore, the conjugate acid C₆H₅NH₃⁺ no longer has this delocalisation (the N is now sp³ and saturated), so it loses the stabilisation that the free amine had. Both effects make phenylamine a much weaker base than ammonia.`},{q:`(d) Predict whether 4-nitrophenylamine is a stronger or weaker base than phenylamine itself, and explain your reasoning.`,marks:2,ans:`4-nitrophenylamine is even WEAKER than phenylamine. The –NO₂ group at the para position is strongly electron-withdrawing both inductively and through resonance: it removes electron density from the ring AND draws the N lone pair further into the ring system (it can be delocalised all the way onto the oxygens of NO₂). The N lone pair is thus even less available, and the base is weakened. Numerically: pKb of 4-nitrophenylamine is around 13 — about 10 000 times weaker than phenylamine.`}]},{title:`Phenylamine and diazonium chemistry`,marks:9,q:`Phenylamine undergoes characteristic reactions with bromine and with nitrous acid.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Describe the reaction of phenylamine with aqueous bromine. State the conditions, write the equation and give a use of this reaction.`,marks:3,ans:`Conditions: simply mix phenylamine with bromine water at room temperature. NO halogen carrier (no FeBr₃ or AlBr₃) is needed. Equation: C₆H₅NH₂ + 3Br₂ → 2,4,6-tribromophenylamine + 3HBr. The orange Br₂(aq) is decolourised; a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromophenylamine forms. Uses: as a confirmatory test for an aromatic 1° amine; also, the resulting tribrominated product is itself useful in further syntheses (the Br atoms can be reduced or substituted).`},{q:`(b) Describe how benzenediazonium chloride is prepared from phenylamine, including the reagents, conditions and the equation. Explain the importance of the temperature condition.`,marks:4,ans:`Reagents: dilute HCl(aq) and sodium nitrite NaNO₂(aq). The two are mixed in situ to generate nitrous acid, HNO₂. Conditions: ICE BATH, with the temperature kept BELOW 10 °C (typically 0–5 °C) throughout. The phenylamine is dissolved in excess HCl as the salt C₆H₅NH₃⁺Cl⁻; cold NaNO₂ solution is added dropwise. Equation: C₆H₅NH₂ + HNO₂ + HCl → C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ + 2H₂O. The temperature constraint is essential because the diazonium salt is THERMALLY UNSTABLE: above 10 °C it decomposes by hydrolysis, C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ + H₂O → C₆H₅OH + N₂ + HCl, giving phenol and nitrogen gas instead of the desired diazonium salt. Below 10 °C the rate of decomposition is slow enough for the salt to be used in a coupling reaction.`},{q:`(c) Show how the cold diazonium salt can be converted into an azo dye. State all reagents, conditions, the equation and explain why the product is coloured.`,marks:2,ans:`Reagents and conditions: prepare phenol dissolved in cold NaOH(aq) (so phenol is present as the more reactive phenoxide ion C₆H₅O⁻). Add this solution to the cold diazonium salt slowly with stirring. Keep the mixture below 10 °C. Equation: C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ + C₆H₅O⁻ → 4-hydroxyazobenzene (HO–C₆H₄–N=N–C₆H₅) + Cl⁻. An orange/red precipitate forms. The colour arises because the molecule contains two benzene rings linked by –N=N–, giving a continuous π-electron system. The HOMO–LUMO gap of this system corresponds to wavelengths in the visible region; the molecule absorbs visible light at one wavelength and reflects/transmits the complementary colour, which we see.`}]},{title:`Amides and proteins`,marks:8,q:`Amides occur as small molecules and as the linkage in proteins.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Write the equation for the preparation of N-methylethanamide (CH₃CONHCH₃) from an acyl chloride. State the conditions and explain why two equivalents of methylamine are used.`,marks:2,ans:`Equation: CH₃COCl + 2CH₃NH₂ → CH₃CONHCH₃ + CH₃NH₃⁺Cl⁻. Conditions: room temperature, often in dry ether or DCM. The reaction is vigorous because the acyl chloride is highly reactive. Two equivalents of methylamine are needed because the first equivalent acts as the nucleophile (forming the amide and releasing HCl), and the second equivalent acts as a base, neutralising the HCl by-product as the methylammonium chloride salt. Without the second equivalent, the released HCl would protonate the amide and the amine, slowing or stopping the reaction.`},{q:`(b) Write equations for the acid hydrolysis and the base hydrolysis of CH₃CONH₂ and state ONE diagnostic observation in each case.`,marks:3,ans:`Acid hydrolysis: CH₃CONH₂ + H₂O + HCl → CH₃COOH + NH₄Cl. Conditions: dilute HCl, reflux 30+ min. Observation: smell of ethanoic acid (vinegar) when the mixture is later cooled and diluted; addition of NaOH at the end releases NH₃ (turns damp red litmus blue) from NH₄Cl. Base hydrolysis: CH₃CONH₂ + NaOH → CH₃COO⁻Na⁺ + NH₃. Conditions: NaOH(aq), reflux 30+ min. Observation: AMMONIA gas evolved during the reaction, detected by its pungent smell and by turning damp red litmus paper blue at the mouth of the condenser.`},{q:`(c) Draw the structure of the dipeptide formed from glycine (H₂N–CH₂–COOH) and alanine (H₂N–CH(CH₃)–COOH) when glycine is the N-terminal residue, and explain why amides (peptide bonds) are surprisingly stable to hydrolysis at body temperature.`,marks:3,ans:`Dipeptide Gly-Ala (glycine N-terminal): H₂N–CH₂–CO–NH–CH(CH₃)–COOH. The peptide bond is the central –CO–NH– section. Stability explanation: the N lone pair of the amide is delocalised into the C=O, giving the C–N bond partial double-bond character. This delocalisation has two consequences for hydrolysis kinetics. (1) The carbonyl C is much less electrophilic than in an acyl chloride or ester, so nucleophilic attack by water is slow. (2) The leaving group (deprotonated amine, RNH⁻ or NH₂⁻) is a very poor leaving group. Both effects mean amide hydrolysis is slow at neutral pH and 37 °C — peptide bonds in proteins survive in the bloodstream. Specific protease enzymes accelerate hydrolysis by ~10⁹-fold but only at chosen sites.`}]},{title:`Amino acid acid-base behaviour`,marks:8,q:`Glycine has pKa₁ = 2.34 and pKa₂ = 9.60.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Identify the two ionisable groups and the pKa value associated with each.`,marks:2,ans:`pKa₁ = 2.34: the –COOH group. Removal of this proton converts –COOH → –COO⁻. pKa₂ = 9.60: the protonated amine –NH₃⁺. Removal of this proton converts –NH₃⁺ → –NH₂. The order matches chemical intuition: a carboxylic acid is more acidic than a protonated alkylammonium, so its proton is lost first as the pH is raised.`},{q:`(b) Calculate the isoelectric point (pI) of glycine and draw the predominant form of glycine at pH = pI.`,marks:2,ans:`For an amino acid with a non-ionisable side chain, pI = (pKa₁ + pKa₂)/2 = (2.34 + 9.60)/2 = 5.97. At pH = pI = 5.97 the predominant form is the ZWITTERION: H₃N⁺–CH₂–COO⁻. The –COOH group is fully deprotonated (pH 5.97 is well above pKa₁ = 2.34); the –NH₃⁺ group is fully protonated (pH 5.97 is well below pKa₂ = 9.60). Net charge = +1 + (−1) = 0.`},{q:`(c) Predict the predominant form of glycine at (i) pH 1, (ii) pH 7, (iii) pH 13. In each case state whether glycine would migrate towards the cathode, the anode, or not at all if placed in an electric field at that pH.`,marks:4,ans:`(i) pH 1: well below pKa₁ = 2.34, so both groups protonated. Form: H₃N⁺–CH₂–COOH. Net charge = +1. Migrates to the CATHODE (negative electrode). (ii) pH 7: above pI 5.97 but below pKa₂ 9.60. The dominant form is still the zwitterion H₃N⁺–CH₂–COO⁻ (net charge ≈ 0); a small fraction is the anion. Net migration is very slight towards the ANODE, but for exam purposes glycine at pH 7 essentially does not migrate (the zwitterion is uncharged overall). (iii) pH 13: well above pKa₂ = 9.60, so both groups deprotonated. Form: H₂N–CH₂–COO⁻. Net charge = −1. Migrates to the ANODE (positive electrode).`}]},{title:`Multi-step synthesis from benzene`,marks:9,q:`Plan the conversion of benzene into 4-hydroxyazobenzene, the orange dye formed by coupling benzenediazonium chloride with phenoxide.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Outline the synthesis as a series of steps. For each step give reagents, conditions, an equation, and the role of the step.`,marks:6,ans:`Step 1: nitrate benzene to nitrobenzene. Reagents: concentrated HNO₃ + concentrated H₂SO₄ (the nitrating mixture). Conditions: 50 °C (warmer would give multiple substitutions). Equation: C₆H₆ + HNO₃ → C₆H₅NO₂ + H₂O. Role: introduces the N-bearing group onto the ring. Step 2: reduce nitrobenzene to phenylamine. Reagents: tin (Sn) + concentrated HCl, reflux; then NaOH(aq) workup. Equations: C₆H₅NO₂ + 6[H] → C₆H₅NH₂ + 2H₂O; the salt formed C₆H₅NH₃⁺Cl⁻ is then liberated by NaOH. Role: converts –NO₂ to –NH₂. Step 3: diazotise phenylamine to benzenediazonium chloride. Reagents: NaNO₂(aq) + dilute HCl(aq), in an ice bath at < 10 °C. Equation: C₆H₅NH₂ + HNO₂ + HCl → C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ + 2H₂O. Role: produces the electrophilic diazonium salt. Step 4: prepare alkaline phenol (separately): dissolve phenol in NaOH(aq) and cool. Phenol is now present as the phenoxide ion C₆H₅O⁻. Step 5: couple the cold diazonium salt with the alkaline phenoxide. Conditions: < 10 °C, mix dropwise. Equation: C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ + C₆H₅O⁻ → 4-hydroxyazobenzene (HO-C₆H₄-N=N-C₆H₅) + Cl⁻. Role: forms the chromophore.`},{q:`(b) For step 3, explain TWO precautions that must be taken to obtain a usable yield of the diazonium salt. For step 5, explain why phenol must be dissolved in NaOH before it is added.`,marks:3,ans:`Step 3 precautions. (i) The temperature must be kept below 10 °C throughout — typically by surrounding the flask with an ice/salt bath. Above 10 °C the diazonium salt rapidly decomposes by reaction with water: C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ + H₂O → C₆H₅OH + N₂ + HCl. So warming destroys the product. (ii) The NaNO₂ should be added slowly and the mixture stirred well — to avoid local heating from the exothermic diazotisation, and to ensure complete consumption of NaNO₂ (excess HNO₂ would attack the diazonium salt or the eventual coupling partner). Step 5 reasoning. Phenoxide ion C₆H₅O⁻ is a much stronger ring activator than neutral phenol C₆H₅OH, because –O⁻ donates a full negative charge into the ring while –OH donates only a lone pair from neutral O. The diazonium ion is a relatively WEAK electrophile (the positive charge is delocalised over the N–N system) and only reacts at a useful rate with rings that are STRONGLY activated. Plain phenol gives little or no coupling at the cold temperature; phenoxide gives fast, clean coupling. The NaOH also keeps the system buffered around pH 9–10 — high enough for phenoxide to dominate, low enough that the diazonium salt is not destroyed by excess hydroxide.`}]}],worked:[{title:`Distinguishing a 1° amine from a 1° amide`,diff:`easy`,q:`A colourless liquid X smells of fish and dissolves to give a basic solution. A colourless liquid Y is odourless, neutral, and on heating with NaOH(aq) gives off a gas that turns damp red litmus blue. Identify X and Y.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Both X and Y contain nitrogen — but their behaviour differs. X is BASIC in water; Y is NEUTRAL but releases a gas with NaOH.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`A primary amine is volatile, has a fishy smell, and gives a basic solution (its lone pair on N grabs H⁺ from water, releasing OH⁻). This matches X.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`An amide is NOT basic (lone pair tied up in C=O delocalisation), is usually a solid or low-volatility liquid, and releases NH₃ on heating with NaOH(aq). This matches Y.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`The gas released from Y is ammonia, NH₃, which turns damp red litmus paper blue.`}],ans:`X is a primary amine (e.g. ethylamine CH₃CH₂NH₂). Y is a primary amide (e.g. ethanamide CH₃CONH₂). The diagnostic split is basicity in water (amine) vs NH₃ release on alkali heating (amide).`},{title:`Predicting basicity`,diff:`medium`,q:`Place the following compounds in order of decreasing basicity and explain your reasoning: (a) ammonia, (b) methylamine, (c) phenylamine, (d) 4-methylphenylamine.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Methylamine: CH₃ donates electrons by +I onto N → lone pair more available → strongest base. (pKb ≈ 3.4.)`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Ammonia: no substituent — neutral baseline. (pKb = 4.75.)`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`4-methylphenylamine: aromatic ring removes some electron density (delocalisation), but the methyl group at the para position pushes electrons back into the ring by +I. The methyl partially compensates for the ring effect, so 4-methylphenylamine is more basic than phenylamine itself but still much less basic than ammonia. (pKb ≈ 8.9.)`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Phenylamine: ring delocalisation of the lone pair, no compensating substituent. Weakest of the four. (pKb ≈ 9.4.)`},{label:`STEP 5`,text:`Order of decreasing basicity: methylamine > ammonia > 4-methylphenylamine > phenylamine.`}],ans:`CH₃NH₂ > NH₃ > 4-CH₃-C₆H₄-NH₂ > C₆H₅NH₂. Alkyl +I increases basicity; aryl ring delocalisation decreases it; a +I substituent on the aryl ring partially recovers the loss.`},{title:`Diazonium salt — yield calculation`,diff:`medium`,q:`0.93 g of phenylamine is dissolved in dilute HCl and diazotised with NaNO₂. The cold diazonium solution is coupled with excess alkaline phenol to give 1.61 g of 4-hydroxyazobenzene (Mr = 198). Calculate the percentage yield.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Mr of phenylamine C₆H₅NH₂ = 6(12) + 7(1) + 14 = 93. Moles of phenylamine = 0.93 / 93 = 0.0100 mol.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Stoichiometry: phenylamine → diazonium → coupling product is 1:1:1. So maximum moles of dye = 0.0100 mol.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Theoretical mass of 4-hydroxyazobenzene = 0.0100 × 198 = 1.98 g.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Actual mass = 1.61 g.`},{label:`STEP 5`,text:`Percentage yield = (1.61 / 1.98) × 100 = 81.3%.`}],ans:`Percentage yield = 81.3%. Typical for this synthesis — losses come from (i) diazonium decomposition before coupling, (ii) purification of the dye.`},{title:`Predict the form of an amino acid at a given pH`,diff:`easy`,q:`Lysine has pKa₁ = 2.18 (–COOH), pKa₂ = 8.95 (α-NH₃⁺) and pKa₃ = 10.53 (side-chain –NH₃⁺). What is its predominant form at pH 7? At pH 12?`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Lysine has THREE ionisable groups: the α-COOH, the α-NH₃⁺, and a side-chain –NH₃⁺ on the long alkyl chain.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`At pH 7: pH > pKa₁ (2.18), so COOH is deprotonated (–COO⁻). pH < pKa₂ (8.95), so α-NH₃⁺ is protonated. pH < pKa₃ (10.53), so side-chain –NH₃⁺ is protonated. Form: H₃N⁺–CH(–CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₂NH₃⁺)–COO⁻. Net charge = +1.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`At pH 12: pH > pKa₁, COOH deprotonated. pH > pKa₂ (8.95), α-NH₃⁺ deprotonated to –NH₂. pH > pKa₃ (10.53), side-chain –NH₃⁺ deprotonated to –NH₂. Form: H₂N–CH(–CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₂NH₂)–COO⁻. Net charge = −1.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Note: for lysine the pI is between the two pKa values flanking the zwitterion (pKa₂ and pKa₃), so pI ≈ (8.95 + 10.53)/2 = 9.74 — much higher than for glycine because lysine has an extra basic group.`}],ans:`At pH 7: cation H₃N⁺–CH(–(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺)–COO⁻, net +1. At pH 12: anion H₂N–CH(–(CH₂)₄–NH₂)–COO⁻, net −1.`},{title:`Identify monomers from a small peptide`,diff:`medium`,q:`Total acid hydrolysis of a tripeptide P gives glycine, alanine and serine in equal molar amounts. Treatment of P with carboxypeptidase (an enzyme that releases the C-terminal residue) gives serine. Treatment with another enzyme that releases the N-terminal residue gives alanine. Deduce the sequence of P.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Total hydrolysis identifies the residues: Gly, Ala, Ser. Three residues; one of each.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Carboxypeptidase identifies the C-terminal residue: Ser. So Ser is at the right-hand end of the chain.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`The N-terminal-releasing enzyme identifies the N-terminus: Ala. So Ala is at the left-hand end.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`The remaining residue Gly must be in the middle.`},{label:`STEP 5`,text:`Sequence: N-terminus → C-terminus: Ala-Gly-Ser. Drawing it out: H₂N-CH(CH₃)-CO-NH-CH₂-CO-NH-CH(CH₂OH)-COOH.`}],ans:`P = Ala-Gly-Ser. Sequencing from both ends inwards is the standard partial-digest approach.`},{title:`Why amide hydrolysis is slow`,diff:`medium`,q:`At room temperature, ethanoyl chloride hydrolyses violently, ethyl ethanoate slowly, and ethanamide barely at all. Account for this trend in terms of structure and electronic effects.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`All three contain the same C=O electrophilic centre. The difference is in the leaving group attached to C=O.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`In ethanoyl chloride, the leaving group is Cl⁻ — a very stable, weak base, an excellent leaving group. The C=O is also strongly polarised because Cl is electron-withdrawing. Result: violent hydrolysis at RT.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`In ethyl ethanoate, the leaving group is RO⁻ (ethoxide), a moderate leaving group; oxygen is more electronegative than nitrogen so ethoxide is a better leaving group than amide-N⁻. The lone pair on the ester O delocalises into the C=O, making the C less electrophilic. Result: slow hydrolysis, requiring acid or base catalysis and reflux.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`In ethanamide, the leaving group would be NH₂⁻, an extremely poor leaving group (very strong base; nitrogen does not stabilise a negative charge). The N lone pair delocalises strongly into the C=O, making C even less electrophilic than in an ester. Result: barely hydrolyses at RT; needs vigorous acid/base + prolonged reflux.`},{label:`STEP 5`,text:`Reactivity order: acyl chloride >> ester > amide. The same trend extends to alkylation/acylation of any nucleophile by these compounds.`}],ans:`Acyl chloride: best leaving group + most polar C=O → fastest. Amide: worst leaving group + most delocalised lone pair → slowest. Ester is intermediate. Both factors (leaving-group ability and electrophilicity of C) work in the same direction across this series.`},{title:`Synthesis: benzene → methyl orange-like dye`,diff:`hard`,q:`Plan the conversion of benzene into the azo dye 4-hydroxyazobenzene HO-C₆H₄-N=N-C₆H₅, giving reagents and conditions for each step.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`We need both an aromatic 1° amine (to diazotise) and a phenol (for coupling). Both can come from benzene.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Make phenylamine. Step 2a: nitrate benzene. C₆H₆ + HNO₃/conc. H₂SO₄ (50 °C) → C₆H₅NO₂. Step 2b: reduce nitrobenzene. C₆H₅NO₂ + Sn/conc. HCl, reflux, then NaOH(aq) → C₆H₅NH₂.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Make phenol (separate batch from benzene). Step 3a: from cumene OR via diazotisation of phenylamine itself: C₆H₅NH₂ → C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ → C₆H₅OH (warm in water, > 10 °C). For our exam answer we can simply state "phenol is available" or use the diazonium-hydrolysis route.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Diazotise phenylamine. C₆H₅NH₂ + NaNO₂ + HCl in ice bath < 10 °C → C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻.`},{label:`STEP 5`,text:`Dissolve phenol in NaOH(aq) and chill. This converts phenol to phenoxide, the active coupling partner.`},{label:`STEP 6`,text:`Couple. Add the cold alkaline phenoxide to the cold diazonium solution (or vice versa) below 10 °C. Reaction: C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ + C₆H₅O⁻ → HO-C₆H₄-N=N-C₆H₅. The orange dye precipitates.`}],ans:`Benzene → nitrobenzene (HNO₃/H₂SO₄, 50 °C) → phenylamine (Sn/conc. HCl, then NaOH) → benzenediazonium chloride (NaNO₂/HCl, < 10 °C). Separately: phenol + NaOH → phenoxide. Couple cold to give 4-hydroxyazobenzene (orange).`},{title:`Calculate amino acid composition from titration`,diff:`hard`,q:`A 0.150 g sample of pure glycine (Mr = 75) is dissolved in water and titrated with 0.100 mol/dm³ NaOH. The first equivalence point is reached after 20.0 cm³ of NaOH has been added. Calculate the moles of glycine and explain which proton has been removed at the first equivalence point.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Moles of glycine = 0.150 / 75 = 2.00 × 10⁻³ mol.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Moles of NaOH used to first equivalence point = 0.100 × 20.0/1000 = 2.00 × 10⁻³ mol.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Ratio: 1 mol NaOH per 1 mol glycine. So the first equivalence point removes ONE proton per glycine.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Glycine in pure water is essentially the zwitterion H₃N⁺–CH₂–COO⁻. As we add NaOH, the more acidic group (–NH₃⁺, pKa₂ = 9.60) is deprotonated next. (The –COOH was already deprotonated to –COO⁻ in the zwitterion.)`},{label:`STEP 5`,text:`After 1 equivalent of NaOH: H₃N⁺–CH₂–COO⁻ + OH⁻ → H₂N–CH₂–COO⁻ + H₂O. The form is now the anion (net −1).`}],ans:`Moles of glycine = 2.00 × 10⁻³. The first equivalence point removes the proton from –NH₃⁺ (pKa₂ = 9.60), converting the zwitterion to the anion H₂N–CH₂–COO⁻. (If we had STARTED from the cation form by first adding HCl, we would see TWO equivalence points: pKa₁ first, then pKa₂.)`}],tutorPrompts:[`Walk me through the basicity argument with examples`,`Why does phenylamine react with bromine without a halogen carrier?`,`Explain the temperature requirement for diazotisation`,`Show me how to plan a synthesis of an azo dye from benzene`,`How do I predict the form of an amino acid at any pH?`,`Compare amide hydrolysis with ester hydrolysis`]};export{e as NORGANIC};